{"causes":["A historical context marked by the decline of feudalism and the rise of nation-states in Europe during the late 20th century.","The influence of J.R.R. Tolkien's "The Lord of the Rings" on the fantasy genre, which George R. R. Martin acknowledged as a significant inspiration for his work.","The cultural phenomenon of geek culture and fan fiction, which has helped to popularize epic fantasy novels among new audiences."],"effects":["A significant impact on the development of modern fantasy literature, influencing numerous authors and works in the genre.","The creation of a global fan base and community centered around "A Clash of Kings" and its sequels, with fans worldwide engaging in fan fiction, cosplay, and other creative expressions.","The adaptation of "A Clash of Kings" into a successful television series, which has further expanded the novel's reach and popularity among new audiences."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"george-r-r-martin","sourceName":"George R. R. Martin","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-clash-of-kings","targetName":"A Clash of Kings","context":"The novel is a direct product of his creative genius."},{"sourceSlug":"jrr-tolkien","sourceName":"J.R.R. Tolkien","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-clash-of-kings","targetName":"A Clash of Kings","context":"Tolkien's influence on fantasy literature is evident in Martin's work."},{"sourceSlug":"geek culture","sourceName":"Geek Culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-clash-of-kings","targetName":"A Clash of Kings","context":"The rise of geek culture has helped to popularize epic fantasy novels among new audiences."}],"places":[{"name":"Westeros","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Essos","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Northern Europe","role":"Geographic Inspiration"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Clash of Kings","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-clash-of-kings"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q300370","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Clash_of_Kings"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Clash of Kings contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Clash of Kings is the second novel in George R. R. Martin's A Song of Ice and Fire epic fantasy series, published on 16 November 1998 by Bantam Books — with over 9 million copies sold worldwide and consistently ranked among the best-selling epic fantasy novels of the late 20th century. Set in Westeros following the death of King Robert Baratheon at the end of A Game of Thrones (1996), A Clash of Kings depicts the War of the Five Kings — the four-way civil war in which Robb Stark (King in the North), Stannis Baratheon (King by right), Renly Baratheon (King by popularity), Joffrey Baratheon (King in King's Landing), and Balon Greyjoy (King of the Iron Islands) simultaneously contest for the Iron Throne. The novel introduces the major characters of Stannis Baratheon and the red priestess Melisandre, continues the arc of Daenerys Targaryen with her dragons in Essos, and depicts the Battle of the Blackwater (the climactic naval battle in which Stannis's attack on King's Landing is repulsed by wildfire) — one of the most praised battle sequences in the series.

A Clash of Kings is notable in the series for its elaboration of the supernatural and religious dimensions of the narrative — the resurrection of the religion of R'hllor (the Lord of Light) through Melisandre, the growing power of the White Walkers beyond the Wall as experienced by Jon Snow, and the reawakening of the dragons (now flying and fire-breathing) in Daenerys's arc. The novel's political complexity — the multi-sided civil war in which every claimant has legitimate and illegitimate arguments — continues Martin's rejection of Tolkienian moral clarity and his historically-grounded exploration of the Machiavellian dynamics of power.

A Clash of Kings was adapted as the second season of the HBO series Game of Thrones (2012), which included the spectacular Battle of the Blackwater episode (directed by Neil Marshall) — widely praised as one of the finest battle sequences in television history. The HBO adaptation substantially raised the novel's profile and contributed to the global phenomenon that made the Game of Thrones franchise the most watched television drama in history.","subjects":["Fantasy Fiction","Modern Era","George R. R. Martin","Epic Fantasy","War of the Five Kings","21st Century","American Literature","Television Adaptation"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Clash of Kings has had a significant impact on the development of modern fantasy literature, influencing numerous authors and works in the genre.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
